--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/cosmos_droid_trajectories.md
@@ -0,0 +1,157 @@
+# Cosmos-DROID Training Trajectories
+
+RoboLab can write successful Cosmos3 rollouts directly as a LeRobot v3 dataset
+for DROID joint-position policy training. This path is separate from RoboLab's
+normal evaluation output:
+
+- `episode_results.jsonl` reports evaluation outcomes;
+- `success/` contains the training rows and H.264 camera streams;
+- HDF5 and JSON sidecars retain simulator state needed for reproduction and
+  debugging, but are not the primary training format.
+
+## Install
+
+Install the optional trajectory dependencies in the same Python 3.11
+environment as RoboLab:
+
+```bash
+uv sync --extra isaac50 --extra trajectory
+```
+
+Isaac Sim pins `packaging==23.0`, while the validated LeRobot fork declares
+`packaging>=24.2`. RoboLab's `uv` override keeps Isaac Sim's required version;
+the LeRobot v3 dataset APIs used here are covered by the recorder tests on that
+version. Keep the pinned LeRobot commit until this compatibility constraint is
+resolved upstream.
+
+## Configure
+
+Copy and edit `examples/configs/droid_lerobot_trajectory.yaml`. At minimum,
+set `checkpoint` to the exact Hugging Face model ID, local bundle digest, or
+other immutable identifier used by the policy server. The recorder rejects an
+unset checkpoint.
+
+The training contract is fixed to match Cosmos-DROID:
+
+| Field | Value |
+| --- | --- |
+| RGB | left shoulder, right shoulder, and wrist |
+| Image format | `640x360`, RGB `uint8`, H.264, 15 FPS |
+| State | 7 joint positions, 1 gripper position, 6 Cartesian values |
+| Action | 7 joint-position commands, 1 gripper command |
+| Alignment | `observation[t] -> executed_action[t]` |
+| Selection | successful episodes only |
+
+The recorder converts RoboLab's gripper convention to raw DROID once. Cosmos'
+DROID loader performs its usual single transform when reading the result.
+
+RoboLab keeps the media writer and the Cosmos-DROID preset separate:
+
+- `robolab/core/logging/lerobot_trajectory.py` provides configurable stream
+  mappings, feature schemas, image sizes, FPS, codec, quality, encoder threads
+  per stream, queue size, and FFmpeg options for custom LeRobot recorders.
+- `droid_lerobot_trajectory.py` supplies the three DROID cameras and the fixed
+  state/action schema above. Its YAML rejects other resolutions, frame rates,
+  and codecs because the result is intended for the current Cosmos loader.
+
+Use the generic layer when building a different dataset contract. The
+`--trajectory-config` CLI currently selects the Cosmos-DROID preset.
+
+## Collect
+
+Start the Cosmos Lite server normally, then pass the YAML to RoboLab:
+
+```bash
+python policies/cosmos3/run.py \
+  --task BananaOnPlateTask \
+  --num-envs 10 --num-runs 1 \
+  --remote-host 127.0.0.1 --remote-port 8500 \
+  --video-mode none --headless --device cuda:0 \
+  --output-folder-name droid_collection \
+  --trajectory-config examples/configs/droid_lerobot_trajectory.yaml
+```
+
+`--video-mode none` disables the human-viewable evaluation video; it does not
+disable the three training camera streams. Do not add `--record-image-data`
+unless full-resolution images are also required in the HDF5 sidecar. The
+LeRobot recorder already captures its own bounded `640x360` streams.
+
+Write the live output to node-local NVMe. Move completed datasets to shared
+storage only after validation.
+
+## Output
+
+With the default `output_root: null`, output is placed under the evaluation
+directory:
+
+```text
+droid_plus_lerobot_640x360_20260412/
+|-- success/                         # LeRobot v3 training dataset
+|   |-- data/                        # aligned state/action Parquet rows
+|   |-- videos/                      # three H.264 streams
+|   `-- meta/                        # info, stats, tasks, episodes
+|-- sidecars/
+|   |-- env_configs/
+|   |-- success/                     # JSON + per-episode HDF5
+|   `-- failure/                     # JSON metadata only when unavailable
+`-- manifest.json
+```
+
+An interrupted batch publishes no partial successful episode. Failed episode
+videos are discarded. `keep_failure_sidecars` controls whether lightweight
+failure metadata is retained.
+
+## Validate
+
+Validate every completed shard before training or transfer:
+
+```bash
+robolab-validate-droid-trajectory \
+  output/droid_collection/droid_plus_lerobot_640x360_20260412
+```
+
+The validator checks the LeRobot schema, continuous frame/global indexes,
+timestamps, video frame counts, successful-only metadata, required sidecars,
+and exact action agreement with HDF5. From a Cosmos environment, also exercise
+the actual training loader:
+
+```bash
+robolab-validate-droid-trajectory DATASET --check-cosmos-loader
+```
+
+## Throughput And Memory
+
+The recorder resizes all three camera batches on the simulator GPU, performs
+one CPU transfer per vector step, and feeds bounded asynchronous H.264 writers.
+No image history remains on the simulator GPU. A four-frame queue per camera
+was selected because a 16-frame queue did not improve throughput and increased
+host memory. Image statistics sample every fourth temporal frame by default;
+all RGB frames are still encoded. On the 1,421-frame validation dataset, this
+changed per-channel means by at most `0.00011`, standard deviations by at most
+`0.00006`, and quantiles by at most one 8-bit value.
+
+The fixed-window A/B below used one RTX 4090 simulator, 10 active environments
+for exactly 128 vector steps, a separate RTX 4090 Edge GenW8A8 server, and no
+evaluation video. Every row used the same initial state and policy setup.
+
+| Training trajectory | Stats frame stride | Wall time | Vector steps/s | Recorder time |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| Disabled | - | 108.20 s | 1.18 | - |
+| Enabled | 1 | 125.04 s | 1.02 | 8.33 s |
+| Enabled (default) | 4 | 120.14 s | 1.07 | 7.71 s |
+
+The default recorder added `11.0%` wall time for three compressed training
+streams, down from `15.5%` with full-frame statistics. Temporal subsampling
+affects LeRobot image statistics only; every RGB frame remains in the videos.
+CPU load, local-disk bandwidth, scene complexity, active environment count,
+and trajectory length can change the result. Run the fixed-window A/B on the
+target collection host before scaling to several lanes.
+
+Leave CPU scheduling to the operating system for a single simulator process.
+When several simulator processes share a host, assign each process a wide,
+disjoint CPU/NUMA set and benchmark the complete lane. Narrow process affinity
+also constrains its FFmpeg children and can slow both simulation and encoding.
+
+A complete Banana-on-plate run produced 9 successful episodes, 1,421 aligned
+frames, and 27 videos in 37 MB at `crf: 23`. H.264 size varies with visual
+complexity and episode length, so size the target task before a large run.
